failed payment recovery is usually calculated by:

{noformat}1 - (sum_final_failed_payment / sum_initial_failed_payment){noformat}

if we apply this logic, _retry_recovery_ will look better than _balance_recovery_. but that’s a deceiving logic due to the fact that for _retry_recovery_ we also need to recover the amount that we had authorized. therefore, I came up with a variable called *_adjusted_recovery_* which will be used as the main indicator to point out which flow has better recovery. 

adjusted recovery:

{noformat}1 - (sum_final_failed_payment / sum_price_difference){noformat}

where price_difference is the difference between {{capture_amount}} and {{preauth_amount}}

price_difference refers to the amount that we need to chase. when we decide to {{keep_authorised_amount}} then price_difference will be the only amount that has to recover. but when we decide to {{cancel_and_create_new}} then we need to recover (price_difference + preauth_amount). 

when looking at the results *on cell 2* for all payments we had an adjustment attempt failed with error_code 328, we see that _retry_recovery_ is performing much worse than _balance_recovery_. I say that it’s performing because it’s too soon to say that payments won’t recover anymore. will have to re-run the numbers later on. as of now: *(last updated 23/07/24)*

* _balance_recovery_ has an adjusted recovery rate of 46%
* _retry_recovery_ has an adjusted recovery rate of *-36%*. the negative value means we’re accruing more debt than we would’ve needed to recover if we decided to {{keep_authorised_amount}}

