Hi [~accountid:712020:a7a7d4bc-10ca-44a0-a22e-fa8752330abd], I looked into all the cases in the UK that we were able to track so far.

* {color:#0747a6}*A list of phishing instances that we know of: links to beehive, if and what data was affected, was any financial damage done*{color}
** See in this sheet: [https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1BJxGidyoUnSg4mOfsqkv07Otp03WLYwIDwgSKhEd85Y/edit?gid=1258211429#gid=1258211429|https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1BJxGidyoUnSg4mOfsqkv07Otp03WLYwIDwgSKhEd85Y/edit?gid=1258211429#gid=1258211429|smart-link]. As for the data affected, there is also a tab with a detailed summary per each BH ticket but in general: while waiting to be picked up, riders are asked for their phone number and 4-digit code to confirm the ride. From what passengers shared so far, it seems that the codes are then used to bypass the 2-factor verification of their email address or Bolt/Uber app. 
** As for driver data - we saw changes in phone number and email (to be able to log into the account) and bank details (for this a bank statement needs to be uploaded - unfortunately a document that’s very easy to tamper with). With access to the driver account, scammers can also access other driver details through the driver portal (although they cannot change them) - this includes Name, address, DOB, NIN, driver’s licence number, passenger invoices.
* {color:#0747a6}*Based on this, we need to receive information on how the phishing happened:*{color}
** riders - as described above
** drivers - some drivers reported that they received a message from a passenger saying “you are about to pick up a VIP passenger, please confirm your phone number and the 4-digit code”. Compromised driver accounts are found either through reports from passengers or they claim they have no idea how the details got changed, so it’s hard to track how exactly the phishing happened.
** No involvement from Bolt CS as far as I know
* {color:#0747a6}*Are we able to block the affected drivers' accounts and request them to contact us by coming to driver hubs to verify their identity?*{color}
** Currently, we don’t ask drivers to come to the hub but we use the Onfido selfie verification to verify their identity. We block them as soon as any report comes in, HP team calls them to verify the correct details and devices over the phone, and blocks any new devices. Once all done, we unblock the account with a scheduled selfie verification.
** We can adjust the process and require a hub visit if required. We would have issues in cities where we don’t have a permanent hub, however, at the moment it seems to be targeted at London drivers specifically

{color:#0747a6}*Re your previous question about Bank details changes:*{color}

We do require drivers to submit a bank statement in order to change their bank details. Unfortunately, bank statements are easy to tamper with and we already saw some fraudulent bank statements submitted that bypassed our document checks and resulted in changing the bank details and therefore payouts to (most probably) scammers' account. We used these cases to inform/educate the teams and keep our compliance team on high alert, but the approach is only reactive atm. 

{color:#0747a6}*Communication from CS*{color}

You can also see in the above-linked document what is currently communicated to riders (the most common example is something along these lines “_Your case was escalated to us on the senior team, and we have taken ownership to ensure that everything is followed up on and actioned. I am closing the ticket now, and taking care of this internally, please do report such instances again to us when needed_" ). Would you be able to advise/draft what CS should communicate to riders/drivers when they report such cases?

